# DTF tier manifest: backup-scoped-restore  (artifact-keeper#5884)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single.
#
# THE BUG (#5884)
# A backup limited to some repositories (`repository_ids`, #2772) archived
# every `repositories` row and every `download_statistics` row of the server.
# Only `artifacts` and the #5654 content tables followed the scope. A restore
# of such an archive therefore brought back, as empty rows, repositories the
# backup was never meant to cover (any that had been deleted since), tried to
# insert download statistics for artifacts the archive does not carry, and,
# when an out-of-scope RPM repository had a repodata depth, ran as a strict
# (depth-bearing) restore that failed as a whole on such a row.
# The fix archives the repository rows in scope and the download statistics
# of the archived artifacts only.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (SETUP)   two generic repositories, IN and OUT, each with one
#             random-content artifact that is downloaded once (so each has a
#             download_statistics row); a full backup scoped to IN completes.
#   (FIX)     archive   the archive's database/repositories.json holds IN's row
#                       only, and database/download_statistics.json only the
#                       download of IN's artifact. Pre-fix: OUT's row and its
#                       download are in the archive too.
#   (FIX)     restore   OUT and then IN are deleted (API, async delete job).
#                       The restore answers 200 `completed` with no errors, IN
#                       is back and its artifact downloads with its sha256, and
#                       OUT stays deleted. Pre-fix: OUT comes back, empty.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: admin calls back to back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
